.TH PERLTHON 3 "May 2026" "perlthon 0.1.0" "Perlthon Python Library"
.SH NAME
perlthon \- natively import and run Perl modules from Python
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B import perlthon
.PP
.B perlthon.use(\fImodule_name\fP)
.B perlthon.eval(\fIcode\fP)
.B perlthon.call(\fIfunction_name\fP, *\fIargs\fP)
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B perlthon
is a Python library that embeds a Perl interpreter, allowing you to load
and call Perl modules directly from Python. It is implemented in Rust using
PyO3, with a C glue layer wrapping Perl's embedding API.
.PP
The library handles automatic type conversion between Python and Perl types
and supports loading XS (compiled C) Perl modules via DynaLoader.
.SH FUNCTIONS
.TP
.BI "perlthon.use(" module_name ")"
Load a Perl module (equivalent to Perl's
.BR use ).
Returns a
.B PerlModule
proxy object. The module is loaded idempotently; calling
.B use()
multiple times for the same module is safe.
.TP
.BI "perlthon.eval(" code ")"
Evaluate a string of Perl code and return the result, converted to a Python
type. Raises
.B RuntimeError
if the Perl code fails.
.TP
.BI "perlthon.call(" function_name ", " *args ")"
Call a Perl function by its fully qualified name (e.g.,
.BR POSIX::floor ).
Arguments are converted from Python to Perl automatically.
.SH PERLMODULE PROXY
The
.B PerlModule
object returned by
.B perlthon.use()
supports attribute access for calling functions in the module's namespace:
.PP
.nf
posix = perlthon.use("POSIX")
posix.floor(3.7)    # calls POSIX::floor(3.7)
.fi
.PP
For OO-style method dispatch (passing the module name as invocant), use the
.B call()
method:
.PP
.nf
module.call("method", arg1, arg2)
.fi
.SH TYPE MAPPING
.TP
.B str
Perl string scalar
.TP
.B int
Perl integer scalar (IV)
.TP
.B float
Perl float scalar (NV)
.TP
.B bool
Perl \f(CW$true\fP / \f(CW$false\fP
.TP
.B None
Perl undef
.TP
.B list
Perl array reference
.TP
.B dict
Perl hash reference
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Evaluate arithmetic:
.PP
.nf
>>> import perlthon
>>> perlthon.eval("2 + 2")
4
.fi
.PP
String concatenation:
.PP
.nf
>>> perlthon.eval('"hello " . "world"')
\(aqhello world\(aq
.fi
.PP
Use a module:
.PP
.nf
>>> posix = perlthon.use("POSIX")
>>> posix.floor(3.7)
3.0
>>> posix.ceil(3.2)
4.0
.fi
.PP
Complex data structures:
.PP
.nf
>>> perlthon.eval('[1, 2, 3]')
[1, 2, 3]
>>> perlthon.eval('{"key" => "value"}')
{\(aqkey\(aq: \(aqvalue\(aq}
.fi
.SH PREREQUISITES
.IP \(bu 2
Python >= 3.12
.IP \(bu 2
Perl development headers (libperl-dev)
.IP \(bu 2
Rust toolchain (for building from source)
